<H2><A NAME="SECTION00064000000000000000">
Deterministic PDAs</A>
</H2>

<P>
We have introduced PDAs as nondeterministic machines which may have
several possibilities how to continue. We now define deterministic
pushdown automata (DPDA) as those which never have a choice. 

<P>
To be precise we say that a PDA <!-- MATH
 $P=(Q,\Sigma,\Gamma,\delta,q_0,Z_0,F)$
 -->
<IMG
 WIDTH="178" HEIGHT="31" ALIGN="MIDDLE" BORDER="0"
 SRC="img445.png"
 ALT="$ P=(Q,\Sigma,\Gamma,\delta,q_0,Z_0,F)$">
is deterministic (is a DPDA) iff
<!-- MATH
 \begin{displaymath}
|\delta(q,x,z)|+|\delta(q,\epsilon,z)| \leq 1
\end{displaymath}
 -->
<P></P>
<DIV ALIGN="CENTER">
<IMG
 WIDTH="186" HEIGHT="31" ALIGN="MIDDLE" BORDER="0"
 SRC="img491.png"
 ALT="$\displaystyle \vert\delta(q,x,z)\vert+\vert\delta(q,\epsilon,z)\vert \leq 1 $">
</DIV><P></P>
Remember, that <IMG
 WIDTH="27" HEIGHT="31" ALIGN="MIDDLE" BORDER="0"
 SRC="img492.png"
 ALT="$ \vert X\vert$"> stands for the number of elements in a finite
set <IMG
 WIDTH="19" HEIGHT="14" ALIGN="BOTTOM" BORDER="0"
 SRC="img493.png"
 ALT="$ X$">.

<P>
That is: a DPDA may get stuck but it has never any choice.

<P>
In our example the automaton <IMG
 WIDTH="21" HEIGHT="29" ALIGN="MIDDLE" BORDER="0"
 SRC="img494.png"
 ALT="$ P_0$"> is not deterministic, e.g. we have
<!-- MATH
 $\delta(q_0,0,\#)=\{(q_0,0\#)\}$
 -->
<IMG
 WIDTH="166" HEIGHT="31" ALIGN="MIDDLE" BORDER="0"
 SRC="img495.png"
 ALT="$ \delta(q_0,0,\char93 )=\{(q_0,0\char93 )\}$"> and
<!-- MATH
 $\delta(q_0,\epsilon,\#)=\{(q_1,\#)\}$
 -->
<IMG
 WIDTH="156" HEIGHT="31" ALIGN="MIDDLE" BORDER="0"
 SRC="img496.png"
 ALT="$ \delta(q_0,\epsilon,\char93 )=\{(q_1,\char93 )\}$"> and hence 
<!-- MATH
 $|\delta(q_0,0,\#)|+|\delta(q_0,\epsilon,\#)| = 2$
 -->
<IMG
 WIDTH="208" HEIGHT="31" ALIGN="MIDDLE" BORDER="0"
 SRC="img497.png"
 ALT="$ \vert\delta(q_0,0,\char93 )\vert+\vert\delta(q_0,\epsilon,\char93 )\vert = 2$">.

<P>
Unlike the situation for finite automata, there is in general no way
to translate a nondeterministic PDA into a deterministic one. Indeed,
there is no DPDA which recognizes the language <IMG
 WIDTH="15" HEIGHT="14" ALIGN="BOTTOM" BORDER="0"
 SRC="img152.png"
 ALT="$ L$"> !
<B>Nondeterministic PDAs are more powerful than deterministic
 PDAs.</B>

<P>
However, we can define a similar language <IMG
 WIDTH="19" HEIGHT="15" ALIGN="BOTTOM" BORDER="0"
 SRC="img498.png"
 ALT="$ L'$"> over
<!-- MATH
 $\Sigma=\{0,1,\$\}$
 -->
<IMG
 WIDTH="91" HEIGHT="31" ALIGN="MIDDLE" BORDER="0"
 SRC="img499.png"
 ALT="$ \Sigma=\{0,1,\$\}$"> which can be recognized
by a deterministic PDA:
<!-- MATH
 \begin{displaymath}
L' = \{ w\$w^R \mid w \in \{0,1\}^* \}
\end{displaymath}
 -->
<P></P>
<DIV ALIGN="CENTER">
<IMG
 WIDTH="189" HEIGHT="36" ALIGN="MIDDLE" BORDER="0"
 SRC="img500.png"
 ALT="$\displaystyle L' = \{ w\$w^R \mid w \in \{0,1\}^* \}$">
</DIV><P></P>
That is <IMG
 WIDTH="19" HEIGHT="15" ALIGN="BOTTOM" BORDER="0"
 SRC="img498.png"
 ALT="$ L'$"> contains palindroms with a marker <IMG
 WIDTH="12" HEIGHT="31" ALIGN="MIDDLE" BORDER="0"
 SRC="img501.png"
 ALT="$ \$$"> in the middle,
e.g. <!-- MATH
 $01\$10 \in L'$
 -->
<IMG
 WIDTH="79" HEIGHT="31" ALIGN="MIDDLE" BORDER="0"
 SRC="img502.png"
 ALT="$ 01\$10 \in L'$">.

<P>
We define a DPDA <IMG
 WIDTH="21" HEIGHT="29" ALIGN="MIDDLE" BORDER="0"
 SRC="img503.png"
 ALT="$ P_1$"> for <IMG
 WIDTH="19" HEIGHT="15" ALIGN="BOTTOM" BORDER="0"
 SRC="img498.png"
 ALT="$ L'$">:
<!-- MATH
 \begin{displaymath}
{P_1} = (\{ q_0,q_1,q_2 \}, \{0,1,\$\},\{0,1,\#\},\delta',q_0,\#,\{q_2\})
\end{displaymath}
 -->
<P></P>
<DIV ALIGN="CENTER">
<IMG
 WIDTH="353" HEIGHT="33" ALIGN="MIDDLE" BORDER="0"
 SRC="img504.png"
 ALT="$\displaystyle {P_1} = (\{ q_0,q_1,q_2 \}, \{0,1,\$\},\{0,1,\char93 \},\delta',q_0,\char93 ,\{q_2\})
$">
</DIV><P></P>
where <IMG
 WIDTH="12" HEIGHT="15" ALIGN="BOTTOM" BORDER="0"
 SRC="img15.png"
 ALT="$ \delta$"> is given by:
<!-- MATH
 \begin{displaymath}
\begin{array}{lcl}
\delta(q_0,x,z) & = & \{(q_0,xz)\} \\
\delta(q_0,\$,z) & = & \{(q_1,z)\} \\
\delta(q_1,x,x) & = & \{(q_1,\epsilon)\} \\
\delta(q_1,\epsilon,\#) & = & \{(q_2,\epsilon)\} \\
\delta(q,x,z) & = & \{\} \qquad \textrm{everywhere else}
\end{array}
\end{displaymath}
 -->
<P></P>
<DIV ALIGN="CENTER">
<IMG
 WIDTH="267" HEIGHT="102" BORDER="0"
 SRC="img454.png"
 ALT="\begin{displaymath}
\begin{array}{lcl}
\delta(q_0,x,z) &amp; = &amp; \{(q_0,xz)\} \\
\...
...a(q,x,z) &amp; = &amp; \{\} \qquad \textrm{everywhere else}
\end{array}\end{displaymath}">
</DIV><P></P>
We can check that this automaton is deterministic. In particular
the 3rd and 4th line cannot overlap because <IMG
 WIDTH="17" HEIGHT="29" ALIGN="MIDDLE" BORDER="0"
 SRC="img505.png"
 ALT="$ \char93 $"> is not an input
symbol. 

<P>
Different to PDAs in general the two acceptance methods are not
equivalent for DPDAs -- acceptance by final state makes it possible
to define a bigger class of langauges. hence, we shall always use
acceptance by final state for DPDAs.

<P>

